---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecureCompressToSize from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfSecureCompressToSize.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="compress-pdf-to-size">
  <PdfSecureCompressToSize client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Type the number the form gave you</h2>
      <p>
        Upload limits are arbitrary: 80 KB, 350 KB, 4 MB, 4.7 MB. Enter the exact figure, and the encoder
        searches for the best quality that fits under it, so you do not need to guess a preset and run
        it again.
      </p>
      <p>
        For round limits, there are separate pages for <a href="/compress-pdf-to-100kb">100 KB</a>,
        <a href="/compress-pdf-to-200kb">200 KB</a>, <a href="/compress-pdf-to-300kb">300 KB</a>,
        <a href="/compress-pdf-to-500kb">500 KB</a>, <a href="/compress-pdf-to-1mb">1 MB</a> and
        <a href="/compress-pdf-to-2mb">2 MB</a>. They use the same engine with the box already filled.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The algorithm, in full</h2>
      <p>These are the steps, so you can predict the result:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Estimate the container. A PDF needs a catalog, a page tree, a cross-reference table and an
          object per page. The tool estimates this as <code>1,200 + 260 × pages</code> bytes and
          subtracts it from your target first.
        </li>
        <li>
          Pick a rendering resolution. Automatic mode divides the remaining budget by the page count
          and converts that to a DPI between 72 and 200. You can override it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Render each page once. This is the expensive step, so it happens only once per page.
        </li>
        <li>
          Calculate the share of the page: remaining budget divided by remaining pages. The share is
          calculated again for each page, so a page that finishes under budget gives the difference
          to the pages after it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Binary-search the quality. Encode at the minimum (5) to check that the page can fit, then
          bisect between 5 and 94 eight times and keep the largest quality whose output is within the
          share.
        </li>
        <li>
          Scale down if the minimum is still too big. The page is drawn again at 70 per cent and the
          search runs again, up to three times.
        </li>
        <li>
          Assemble and measure. The tool reports the final size, the average quality and the decisions
          for each page.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why the search matters more than the encoder</h2>
      <p>
        All tools use the same JPEG encoder. The difference is how many times it can run. An upload-based
        compressor pays for CPU and bandwidth per request, so nine encodes per page cost it money, and
        it gives you three presets. On your own hardware, the ninth attempt costs only a few
        milliseconds.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the same reason, there is no daily quota here.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Choosing a sensible target</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Situation</th>
              <th scope="col">Try</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>A form states a limit</td>
              <td>That limit exactly; the search always finishes under it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Email attachment, no stated limit</td>
              <td>500 KB to 2 MB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Attaching to a chat app</td>
              <td>Under 8 MB covers the Discord free tier and most others</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Posting on a slow connection</td>
              <td>1 MB, and consider grayscale</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Archiving a scan you will read again</td>
              <td>
                Do not use this tool. Use the lossless mode in
                <a href="/compress-pdf">Compress PDF</a> and keep the text layer
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Units, rounding and what "KB" means here</h2>
      <p>
        Kilobytes are binary here: 1 KB is 1,024 bytes and 1 MB is 1,048,576 bytes. Windows Explorer,
        the macOS Get Info panel and almost all upload forms report file size this way. A 100 KB target
        thus means 102,400 bytes, not 100,000.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a form means decimal kilobytes, aim slightly lower (97 KB instead of 100 KB) to be safe with
        both definitions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What this tool will not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Keep the text layer. A byte target requires rendering pages as images. A file that keeps its
          text layer cannot reliably reach a byte target.
        </li>
        <li>
          Improve a bad scan. Compression cannot add detail. A blurry source gives a smaller blurry file.
        </li>
        <li>
          Go below the structural minimum. A valid PDF needs at least a few hundred bytes per page, and
          the result panel tells you when you reach this limit.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
